        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Human space exploration requires astronauts to maintain a high level of cognitive performance in the presence of a range of fatigue-related conditions associated with perturbations of sleep and circadian neurobiology such as slam shifts, excessive cognitive and physical workloads (EVAs), and environmental stressors such as elevated CO2, hypoxia, and radiation. This highlights the critical need for comprehensive, sensitive, and valid neurocognitive assessments in space flight and during exploration type missions. This study assessed cognitive performance of crewmembers who were confined to the Human Exploration Research Analog (HERA) facility for 7-14 days. Crewmembers were exposed to different stressors including high and low workload, sleep deprivation, and time delays. The Cognition test battery consists of 10 validated and brief neuropsychological tests that cover a wide array of cognitive domains and that were specifically developed for high-performing astronauts by the research team. 
<p>
This study had the following specific aims: <br><ol>
<li>Test feasibility of regular Cognition data acquisition in the space analog environment of the HERA.  
<li>Perform descriptive analysis of changes in cognitive performance with time in mission and related to mission stressors. 
<li>Measure rest activity patterns and light exposure with actigraphs (Campaign 1 only)
<li>Test feasibility of proximity data acquisition in HERA (Campaign 1 only). </li></ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The Cognition software was installed on Apple iPads that are already part of the HERA equipment. Before pre-isolation baseline testing, the crewmembers were asked to watch a standardized introductory video that familiarized them with the Cognition software. The crewmembers performed Cognition three times pre-mission, on a daily basis in-mission, and three times post-mission. 
<p>
The crewmembers continuously wore wrist actigraphs to determine rest-activity patterns and light intensity throughout the missions. These data were used to characterize average sleep duration, the placement of sleep periods, the overall level of activity, and the intensity of light exposure depending on the time in mission and mission specific events. 
<p>
Seven proximity beacons were strategically placed throughout the HERA facility (three on floor 1, three on floor 2, and one in the hygiene module). Each crewmember wore one beacon and one receiver continuously throughout the mission. Investigators examined whether they were able to detect where in the facility and with whom crewmembers spent most of their time.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Subjective ratings of tiredness, sleepiness, physical exhaustion, mental fatigue, stress, and workload significantly decreased, while ratings of sleep quality and happiness significantly increased with time in mission. In general, crewmembers rated themselves happy, healthy, not stressed, not depressed, not bored, not lonely, with low levels of monotony and high levels of sleep quality. Ratings of tiredness, workload, sleepiness, physical exhaustion, and mental fatigue were medium to high, but decreased with time in mission. Overall, the observed pattern suggests that, on average, crewmembers coped well with or adjusted well to the isolated and confined HERA environment with increasing time in mission. 
<p>
        </div>

